{
  "image_name": "2fde224f6834ca00be5598ad3789bede.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the coronavirus life cycle inside a host cell and highlights host factors the virus uses or perturbs at each step. The virus first attaches at the plasma membrane through receptors such as ACE2 and can enter either by direct surface fusion involving TMPRSS2 or through endocytosis, where cathepsin L and endosomal machinery appear to assist entry. Several trafficking and endolysosomal factors are marked around these compartments, alongside viral proteins that interact with them.\n\nA central theme is the formation of replication compartments on the endoplasmic reticulum. The viral genome is released, and ER membranes are remodeled into vesicle-like structures that support RNA replication, with PI3K-related proteins, VMP1, and TMEM41B indicated near these membranes. Translation-related host factors are also shown, suggesting support for viral protein synthesis.\n\nOn the right, newly made genomes and structural proteins assemble into nucleocapsids in the ER-Golgi intermediate compartment, then move through the Golgi and lysosomal or secretory pathways for release from the cell. The figure also emphasizes host pathways linked to cholesterol homeostasis, autophagosome-lysosome fusion, and stress granule components, implying that coronaviruses exploit membrane trafficking, lipid regulation, and RNA-handling systems throughout infection.",
